// Hand-curated skills and their per-rules-set categories (skills,
// skill_rules_sets).
//
// Two sections, kept in one file so a skill's existence and its category can
// be reviewed against the same source page side by side:
//
//   skills         -- EVERY skill this file's skillRulesSets section
//                     references, not just the ones that are genuinely
//                     exclusive to CRP, CRP+ or BB2016. This mirrors the
//                     "pre-register so a later upsert matches" pattern
//                     races-and-positions.json5 and
//                     position-characteristics-gap-fill.json5 already use for
//                     races and positions: this file's `before-other-
//                     importers` phase runs as its own, EARLIER invocation
//                     than the BBL/TP import (see docs/import-manual/index.md,
//                     "Run it"), so on a from-empty-database run, a skill like
//                     "Block" or "Dodge" does not exist yet when this file's
//                     skillRulesSets rows are processed --
//                     ExternalIdResolverService.resolve is a pure lookup with
//                     no fallback, so an unregistered skill reference would
//                     silently fail to resolve and drop that category row as
//                     an ImportError. Pre-registering every skill here means
//                     (1) BBL/TP's own later upsert matches onto this same
//                     row via the shared "Name" external id instead of
//                     creating a duplicate, and (2) BBL/TP's own category
//                     read-back (during their run) finds the curated category
//                     row already there.
//   skillRulesSets -- the category table for EVERY skill, whichever importer
//                     introduces it. A missing row means "this rules set does
//                     not have this skill", and the BBL/TP skills imports read
//                     these rows back before recording a starting skill: an
//                     uncurated (skill, rules set) pair is reported as an
//                     import error naming this file.
//
// Every id in both sections is the skill's own display name, which is what
// NameExternalIdService.forSkill produces and what both source importers send.
// A skill whose display name once carried a parenthetical value ("Loner
// (4+)", "Mighty Blow (+1)") is NOT a distinct skill: the value is specific
// to the position that grants it, not to the skill's identity, so it is
// curated as a per-position attributeValue in
// after-other-importers/position-skills.json5 instead -- only the bare skill
// name is registered here.
//
// 'unique' is a category like any other: it is the only marker a star
// player's exclusive skill has -- there is no per-association flag. The CRP,
// CRP+ and BB2016 groups below curate no 'unique' rows: CRP's own "SKILL
// CATEGORIES" table (p.21) has no Extraordinary-vs-unique split, and
// cross-checking the CRP Star Players list (pp.79-80) found no skill there
// that is not already one of the 75 skills the categories table itself
// lists (i.e. no star-player-only skill to mark unique). Every CRP-era
// Extraordinary skill is curated as 'trait' per the task's mapping
// instructions. The BB2020, DB2021 and BB2025 groups DO curate 'unique'
// rows: TP publishes each star's own exclusive skill as a `specialRuleName`
// string, and every distinct value in the downloaded TP data dumps is
// curated as 'unique' for the rules set it was found under.
//
// Sources, this file:
//   CRP -- m750496a_BB_Competition_Rules_Pack.pdf:
//          - p.21, "SKILL CATEGORIES" table -- the category for every skill.
//          - pp.19-21, "SKILL DESCRIPTIONS" -- each skill's own heading
//            confirms its category and exact spelling; used to cross-check
//            the categories table, which lists this skill as "Claw / Claws"
//            (see the Mutation group below for how the two spellings merge).
//          - pp.22-25, "TEAM ROSTERS" -- the 21 core CRP races' starting
//            skills, cross-checked against the categories table; introduced
//            no skill the table did not already list.
//   CRP -- Stunty_Leeg_Handbook_v1_0.pdf, "STUNTY TEAM ROSTERS" (pp.7-16) and
//          "STAR PLAYERS" (pp.17+) -- the 15 Stunty Leeg races and their star
//          players, checked for any skill not already in the CRP categories
//          table. None found: every skill name on these rosters (allowing for
//          this handbook's own inconsistent "Claw"/"Claws" and
//          "Side Step"/"Sidestep" spellings, both merged as one skill each --
//          see the Mutation and Agility groups below -- and a "Regenerate"
//          typo for "Regeneration") already appears in the CRP core book's
//          list, so this file adds no Stunty-Leeg-only `skills` entries.
//
// (CRP+ and BB2016 sources are out of scope for this file -- see a later
// curation task/file for those rules sets' category rows.)
//
// The three "CRP-only" entries below (of the 76 total `skills` entries this
// file curates) were identified by diffing the 76 skill spellings this file
// curates for CRP against every literal skillMaster/skills-cell string
// actually present in the downloaded tools/import-bbl and tools/import-tp
// data dumps (the modern-era source data BBL/TP's own upserts read). Two
// CRP-table skills never appear in either dump under any spelling and are
// curated below. A third near-miss, "Blood Lust", is curated too: TP's data
// only ever spells it "Bloodlust" (one word) -- the same skill under a
// BB2020+ rename, confirmed by the developer, so its `skills` entry lists
// both spellings as externalIds (see the Mutation/Extraordinary groups
// below). The remaining 73 entries are skills BBL/TP can also create; see the
// section header comment above for why they are pre-registered here too.
{
  externalSystems: [
    { name: 'Name', category: 'bookkeeping' },
  ],
  skills: [
